The problem asks us to find a positive integer $N$ such that $N! = X$, given $X$.
The constraints are $2 \le X \le 3 \times 10^{18}$.
It is guaranteed that such an $N$ exists and is unique.

Let's look at the constraints on $X$. The maximum value is $3 \times 10^{18}$.
We need to find $N$. Since $N!$ grows very rapidly, $N$ will not be very large.
Let's estimate the maximum possible value for $N$.
$10! = 3,628,800$
$12! \approx 4.79 \times 10^8$
$13! \approx 6.22 \times 10^9$
$14! \approx 8.71 \times 10^{10}$
$15! \approx 1.3 \times 10^{12}$
$16! \approx 2.09 \times 10^{13}$
$17! \approx 3.55 \times 10^{14}$
$18! \approx 6.40 \times 10^{15}$
$19! \approx 1.21 \times 10^{17}$
$20! \approx 2.43 \times 10^{18}$
$21! \approx 5.10 \times 10^{19}$

Since $X \le 3 \times 10^{18}$, the maximum possible value for $N$ is 20.
Wait, $20! \approx 2.43 \times 10^{18}$, which is less than $3 \times 10^{18}$.
$21! \approx 5.1 \times 10^{19}$, which is greater than $3 \times 10^{18}$.
So $N$ is at most 20.

Since $N$ is small (at most 20), we can simply iterate through integers starting from 1 (or 2) and compute their factorials until we reach $X$.
Alternatively, since we are given $X$ and need to find $N$, we can iterate $N$ from 1 upwards, calculate $N!$, and check if it equals $X$.
Or, since $N$ is small, we can precompute factorials or just compute on the fly.
Given the constraint $X \le 3 \times 10^{18}$, standard 64-bit integers (like Python's arbitrary precision integers) can handle this easily.

